{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And every man whose heart lifted him, etc.</b> The meaning of his saying <b>whose heart lifted him in willingness</b>, etc.: Know that there are two levels among the donors: the first is one who donates of his own free will according to his means and the proportion of his wealth, and this one is called ״נָדְבָה רוּחוֹ״ (״the willingness of his spirit״), to exclude the possibility that he is not doing the deed as though it were a burden upon his soul. The second is one who donates beyond his means, out of the greatness of his heart's goodness, and this one is called ״נְשָׂאוֹ לִבּוֹ״ (״his heart lifted him״), meaning that the heart lifts him and rates him at the level of one wealthier than he is, to give something precious. And therefore it says that among Israel both types here mentioned were found, and it opens with the superior of the two and says ״כָּל אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר נְשָׂאוֹ לִבּוֹ״, and it was precise to use the word <b>אִישׁ</b> — a term of distinction. Corresponding to the second it says <b>and everyone whose spirit moved him in willingness</b>, and it does not say ״אִיש״, for he is not as distinguished as the first. And from this inference you know that among all the donors there were only these two levels, and both are to their praise — that they donated with a full heart and did not do the thing with any lack of willingness of heart, only that one is greater than the other. And therefore it was precise to say <b>they brought the offering of HaShem</b>, to allude to the hidden dimension that He alluded to in His saying (Exodus 35:5) ״יְבִיאֶהָ אֵת תְּרוּמַת ה׳״, as we explained there — that the intention is toward the intelligible realm, and this matter is fundamentally dependent on the willingness of the heart. And behold, here it is." "<b>And for all its service, etc.</b> The verse informs us that all the needs of the Tabernacle — meaning, all the thirteen things required for the Tabernacle, etc. — came themselves as donations, and there was no need to purchase any of them with the silver and gold brought in the offering. Understand this:"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:36:58.316716924Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}